          body:
            "A [[primary-headache|primary headache]] is the disorder itself: migraine, tension-type headache, cluster headache. A [[secondary-headache|secondary headache]] is a symptom of something else, such as bleeding, infection, raised pressure, or inflamed arteries. The International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3) sorts both.\n\nMost headaches seen in practice are primary, so testing everyone is neither possible nor useful. Instead, take a history that actively looks for red flags. The SNNOOP10 list is one way to remember them:\n\nS: Systemic symptoms, including fever or weight loss. Points to meningitis or encephalitis, giant cell arteritis, or cancer.\nN: Neoplasm in the history. Points to brain metastasis.\nN: Neurological deficit or reduced consciousness. Points to stroke, hemorrhage, mass, venous thrombosis, or encephalitis.\nO: Onset sudden, a [[thunderclap-headache|thunderclap headache]] that peaks within a minute. Points to subarachnoid hemorrhage first, then reversible cerebral vasoconstriction, cerebral venous thrombosis, cervical artery dissection, and pituitary apoplexy.\nO: Older age at onset. Points to giant cell arteritis and mass lesions.\nP: Pattern change or a recent new headache. Points to a mass, medication overuse, or another new process.\nP: Positional. Worse lying down and on waking suggests raised intracranial pressure; worse upright and better lying flat suggests low pressure from a CSF leak (after a [[lumbar-puncture|lumbar puncture]] or spontaneously).\nP: Precipitated by sneezing, coughing, straining, or exercise. Points to a posterior fossa lesion or Chiari malformation.\nP: [[papilledema|Papilledema]]. Points to raised intracranial pressure: a mass, venous sinus thrombosis, or idiopathic intracranial hypertension.\nP: Progressive headache or atypical features.\nP: Pregnancy or the puerperium. Points to cerebral venous thrombosis, preeclampsia, pituitary apoplexy, and reversible vasoconstriction.\nP: Painful eye with autonomic features. Points to acute angle-closure glaucoma or a lesion near the cavernous sinus or pituitary, although cluster headache does this too.\nP: Post-traumatic onset. Points to subdural hematoma.\nP: Pathology of the immune system (HIV, immunosuppressive drugs). Points to opportunistic infection or lymphoma.\nP: Painkiller overuse or a new drug. Points to [[medication-overuse-headache|medication overuse headache]] or a drug side effect.\n\nThunderclap headache is managed in the subarachnoid hemorrhage module (c-ich-sah) and venous thrombosis in c-cvt-dissection. The point here is to recognize that the flag is there and send the patient down that pathway.",
