          body:
            "[[giant-cell-arteritis|Giant cell arteritis]] is granulomatous inflammation of large and medium arteries, including the branches of the external carotid artery. It occurs in people aged 50 or more (an absolute requirement in the 2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria). Ask about a new headache, often temporal, scalp tenderness, [[jaw-claudication|jaw claudication]] (jaw pain that comes on with chewing and eases with rest), transient or sudden visual loss, morning shoulder and neck stiffness ([[polymyalgia-rheumatica|polymyalgia rheumatica]]), and fever or weight loss. The temporal artery may be thickened, tender, or pulseless. ESR and CRP are usually raised.\n\nThe danger is ischemia of the optic nerve, which can cause sudden, permanent, and sometimes bilateral blindness. So when giant cell arteritis is suspected, start high-dose glucocorticoids the same day and arrange confirmation afterwards. The 2021 ACR/Vasculitis Foundation guideline favors temporal artery biopsy within about two weeks of starting oral glucocorticoids, a long segment because inflammation can skip parts of the artery, and intravenous pulse glucocorticoids when vision is threatened. Temporal artery ultrasound showing a halo sign is used in some centers and counts toward classification.\n\n[[idiopathic-intracranial-hypertension|Idiopathic intracranial hypertension]] is raised intracranial pressure with no mass, hydrocephalus, or venous thrombosis, most often in young women with obesity or recent weight gain. Clues are a daily headache, often worse lying down, [[transient-visual-obscurations|transient visual obscurations]], pulsatile tinnitus, horizontal double vision from a stretched [[abducens-nerve|abducens nerve]], and bilateral papilledema. The order matters: brain imaging with venography first, to exclude a mass and cerebral venous thrombosis, and only then lumbar puncture, which shows a raised [[opening-pressure|opening pressure]] with normal CSF contents. Untreated, it can cause permanent visual loss.",
