            simpler: "Over 50 with a new headache: think of inflamed arteries, ask about chewing pain and vision, check ESR and CRP, and treat with steroids straight away if it fits. Young with headache and swollen optic discs: scan with venography first, then measure the pressure.",
            mechanism: "In giant cell arteritis, inflammation narrows the vessel lumen. The maxillary artery supplies the chewing muscles (jaw claudication) and the posterior ciliary arteries supply the optic nerve head (arteritic ischemic optic neuropathy). In idiopathic intracranial hypertension, raised CSF pressure is transmitted along the optic nerve sheath and swells the disc.",
            compare: "Both cause headache with visual symptoms, but different ones. Giant cell arteritis threatens sudden loss of vision in one eye from ischemia. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension gives seconds-long greying of vision with posture change and slowly constricting fields from chronic disc swelling.",
