            "[[migraine|Migraine]] without aura, in ICHD-3, is recurrent attacks lasting 4 to 72 hours (untreated) with at least two of: one-sided location, pulsating quality, moderate or severe intensity, and worsening with routine physical activity; and at least one of: nausea or vomiting, or both [[photophobia|photophobia]] and phonophobia. At least five attacks are needed for the diagnosis.\n\nA [[migraine-aura|migraine aura]] is a fully reversible visual, sensory, or speech disturbance. In a typical aura at least three of these hold: one aura symptom spreads gradually over 5 minutes or more; two or more symptoms occur one after another; each symptom lasts 5 to 60 minutes; at least one is one-sided; at least one is positive (something added, such as flashing lights, a [[scintillating-scotoma|scintillating scotoma]], or pins and needles); and headache follows within 60 minutes. Visual aura is the most common. Aura with motor weakness is classified separately (hemiplegic migraine).\n\nAura versus [[transient-ischemic-attack|transient ischemic attack]]: aura usually starts with positive symptoms that spread across the visual field or march up a limb over minutes, and symptoms come one after another. A transient ischemic attack starts suddenly, is maximal at onset, and is usually negative (loss of vision, numbness, weakness) with all symptoms beginning together. A first aura-like episode in an older adult, sudden-onset symptoms, or weakness should be handled as a possible transient ischemic attack.",
